defmodule <%= inspect(@module) %> do
  @moduledoc """
  The example's context: how a page records an entry as the person signed in. It is the pages'
  door onto `<%= inspect(@contract) %>`, as `EhOsContract.Plug` is the door of a request to
  `/api`: it calls `EhOsContract.call/3` with the person's actor, so the engine asks whether they
  may before anything else (`<%= @contract_dir %>/authorization.md`, "Another door"). No page
  builds the command's struct.

  ehos_app wrote it with the example contract, and it is the application's own to change or
  remove, with `<%= inspect(@live) %>`.
  """

<%= if @boundary do %>  # No `use Boundary` of its own: one here would take in the contract's decider,
  # `<%= inspect(@module) %>.Entry`, which the engine calls. It stays in the root's boundary,
  # which exports it to the web.

<% end %>  alias EhOsContract.{Actor, Receipt}

  @doc """
  Records an entry as `actor`: `params` as the form sent them, `"entry_id"` and `"amount"`. The
  id is the form's, a fresh UUID when it opened, and the person can change it: only the contract
  guards an entry that exists. It is the idempotency key too, so a second call with it, once the
  first was accepted, answers the first's receipt and records nothing; a refused call can be made
  again. Refused `:missing_capability` when the actor's credential does not grant
  `<%= @name %>.record_entry`, whatever `params` hold.
  """
  @spec record_entry(Actor.t(), map()) :: {:ok, Receipt.t()} | {:error, term()}
  def record_entry(%Actor{} = actor, params) do
    entry_id = Map.get(params, "entry_id")

    EhOsContract.call(
      {:command, "<%= @name %>.record_entry"},
      %{"entry_id" => entry_id, "amount" => whole_number(Map.get(params, "amount"))},
      actor: actor,
      idempotency_key: "entry:#{entry_id}"
    )
  end

  # A form sends text, and the engine decodes a command's integer from JSON's number alone: a
  # whole number is read as one, and anything else is left for the engine to refuse as invalid,
  # after it asks whether the person may.
  defp whole_number(text) when is_binary(text) do
    case Integer.parse(String.trim(text)) do
      {number, ""} -> number
      _other -> text
    end
  end

  defp whole_number(other), do: other
end
